From 087e2e4f2826dfe67b7aa7dc6eb19ea80793e7bd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: qiyinxi <qiyinxi@foxmail.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 19:53:08 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 13/23] host: hold the FrameBuffer lock while draining a
 RenderThread's GL state

Since "Move GL handling to the GL server" (5010278c, Change-Id
I85b99b352df082daa8a7d7728c15346bb900520e),
FrameBuffer::Impl::drainGlRenderThreadResources() calls
EmulationGl::drainRenderThreadResources() without taking m_lock. Before
that change the work went through drainGlRenderThreadSurfaces() and
drainGlRenderThreadContexts(), which both take it.

EmulationGl::drainRenderThreadSurfaces() binds the shared pbuffer
context through the color buffer ContextHelper, walks mWindows and
closes ColorBuffers via closeColorBufferByWindow(), which expects m_lock
to be held. Every other user of that context binds it under m_lock. So
when a guest process exits, its RenderThreads bind the context
concurrently with each other, with the process cleanup thread and with
ColorBuffer updates, for example YUV frames that the host media decoder
uploads on a vCPU thread. On Windows the second wglMakeCurrent() fails
with ERROR_BUSY; the caller then runs its GL calls with no context
current, and when that is the YUV converter's first frame, the
conversion shader "fails to compile" and GFXSTREAM_FATAL aborts the
emulator.

Take m_lock in drainGlRenderThreadResources(), as the two functions that
it replaced did.

Test: Windows 11 host, NVIDIA GPU, -gpu host; guest H.264 decode to an
      ImageReader in a loop of full, killed and overlapping sessions.
      Unfixed, the emulator aborted after ~310 sessions with the CUDA
      decoder and ~65 with ANDROID_EMU_MEDIA_DECODER_CUDA=0 (ffmpeg).
      With this series, 400 CUDA and 200 ffmpeg sessions ran without
      an abort or an error; earlier versions of it ran 1520 and 707
      more.
Test: with extra logging of the pbuffer context's owner, ~136 ffmpeg
      sessions without this change gave 57 ERROR_BUSY binds, all
      against a draining RenderThread; with it, 0 conflicts.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:Claude-Opus-5.5
Change-Id: I7c35598b87c5844a375fc8e1f6e419ba6c27165b
---
 host/frame_buffer.cpp | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/host/frame_buffer.cpp b/host/frame_buffer.cpp
index f455cb2a..9b015892 100644
--- a/host/frame_buffer.cpp
+++ b/host/frame_buffer.cpp
@@ -4319,13 +4319,18 @@ void FrameBuffer::Impl::createEmulatedEglFenceSync(EGLenum type, int destroyWhen
     }
 }
 
-void FrameBuffer::Impl::drainGlRenderThreadResources() {
+void FrameBuffer::Impl::drainGlRenderThreadResources() NO_THREAD_SAFETY_ANALYSIS {
     // If we're already exiting then snapshot should not contain
     // this thread information at all.
     if (isShuttingDown()) {
         return;
     }
 
+    // Draining binds the shared pbuffer context and closes ColorBuffers, so
+    // it must hold m_lock like every other user of that context. Otherwise
+    // another thread (e.g. a ColorBuffer update from the media decoder)
+    // fails to make the context current and runs its GL calls without one.
+    AutoLock mutex(m_lock);
     if (m_emulationGl) {
         m_emulationGl->drainRenderThreadResources();
     }
